# Virtual environment
.venv/
venv/
ENV/

# Python caching
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class

# User configurations (private keys & tokens)
config/api_keys.json
config/google_token.json

# The WHOLE certs directory, not an extension list.
#
# This was `config/certs/*.key` and `*.crt`, which is the same partial-family
# mistake that put memory/long_term.bak in a public repo: the rule named the
# extensions someone thought of. core/prompt.txt's own operational rule names
# ".key, .pem" and a .pem was not covered — nor .p12, .pfx, .jks, or a key
# with no extension at all. Nothing in this directory is ever publishable, so
# the directory is the rule.
config/certs/

# Environment files. Named in core/prompt.txt's refuse-list and ignored
# nowhere, which is the whole of how a secret file reaches a commit.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Backups and half-written files. `memory/*` covers the ones that actually
# leaked, but a .bak is a copy of whatever it was next to, and the rule should
# not depend on where the copy landed.
*.bak
*.tmp


# Local databases and runtime state.
#
# Every file in memory/ that is not code is the user's private life: names of
# family and partners, habits, projects. install.sh git-clones into
# ~/.aethelark, so every installation is a git checkout and anything not
# ignored here is one `git add -A` away from being published. Naming
# long_term.json alone was not enough - memory_manager.py writes a sibling
# long_term.bak on every save, and that .bak reached the public repo with real
# personal data in it.
#
# So: ignore the whole directory and re-admit the code explicitly. A new
# runtime file cannot leak by being forgotten; it has to be allow-listed.
memory/*
!memory/*.py
memory/__pycache__/

# Whatever the user drops into the dashboard. dashboard/server.py creates
# BASE_DIR/uploads at runtime, so these are their files, in a git checkout,
# for the same reason memory/ was.
uploads/
dashboard/__pycache__/
config/__pycache__/
actions/__pycache__/

# Coverage artifacts. Written into the repo root by `pytest --cov`.
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/

# IDEs & System files
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store
scratch/

# Secrets — OAuth tokens & TLS keys (must never be committed)
config/google_token.json
config/certs/*.key
config/certs/*.crt

# debug screenshots
screenshot_*.png

# agent workspaces: worktrees and per-plan scratch. Both are scratch that must
# never be committed back into the repository they are checked out from.
.claude/worktrees/
.superpowers/

# Screenshots the vision tool writes into the repo root — these are
# pictures of the user's actual screen and must never be committed.
screenshot*.png

# Internal developer docs — architecture, specs, plans, roadmap, audits.
# This repo is PUBLIC (curl-install distribution): only genuinely
# user-facing docs (readme.md, Aethelark_Google_Setup.md,
# packaging/README.md) belong in the tracked tree. Everything that exists
# to help build the codebase, not use it, stays local-only.
Aethelark_Architecture.md
Aethelark_Specifications.md
Aethelark_Vision.md
CURRENT_STATUS.md
# docs/ was a blanket ignore, which hid the module contract from the code it
# describes: seven card keys were added on 2026-09-03 and the interface that
# documents them could not be committed in the same change. An untracked
# contract is not a contract, it is a note.
#
# `docs/*` rather than `docs/`, because git does not descend into an excluded
# directory and the negations below would never be reached.
docs/*
# The product, in the operator's own words, dated and attributed. This outranks
# BEHAVIOR_SPEC.md, which an AI generated and whose rules mostly trace to
# nothing. A vision that cannot travel with the code is not a vision, it is a
# conversation nobody can find again.
# The interface a module author writes against. Must version with the card.
!docs/MODULE_CONTRACT.md
# What the product must do, and how to check it. These two are the spec a test
# author works from and are useless apart.
!docs/BEHAVIOR_SPEC.md
!docs/TEST_SETUP.md
# The 2.0 half of the spec, split out of BEHAVIOR_SPEC.md on 2026-09-04 so a
# session reading the 1.0 spec does not pay to read unbuilt sections too.
!docs/BEHAVIOR_SPEC_2.0.md
#
# These three were local while the repository was public: SHIP_1.0.md itemises
# where the product currently says untrue things, TWO_POINT_OH.md is unshipped
# strategy, and DECISIONS.md carries candid assessments. The repository went
# private on 2026-09-03, and being untracked meant nothing could recover them
# from an over-eager edit. Tracked as of 2026-09-04 so they can be trimmed
# reversibly. If this repository is ever made public again, these three come
# out first.
!docs/SHIP_1.0.md
!docs/DECISIONS.md
!docs/TWO_POINT_OH.md

# Internal handoff/state notes — same rule as the docs above: this exists to
# help build the codebase, not to install or use it. Public repo.
HANDOFF.md

# Rewrite planning notes. Names the upstream author and the inherited line
# count, and describes commercial and repo-migration plans — none of which is
# something a person installing this needs, and all of which is something the
# public should not be handed. Local-only.
NON_DERIVATIVE_ROADMAP.txt
Module_Software_Factory.txt

# Session logs. CLAUDE.md tells anyone debugging this to run
#   eagle > ~/eagle.log 2>&1
# and then read it — those logs are the tool that found nearly every real bug
# here. The path in that instruction is ~, but the natural place to run it from
# is the repository, and a redirect does not care which directory it is in.
#
# A voice-session log is the entire conversation: what the user said, what the
# eagle said back, every tool argument, and whatever an API error quoted back
# at us. Nothing of the sort is tracked today, and CLAUDE.md records that
# internal material has reached git history here once already. That was
# recoverable only because the repository was private at the time.
*.log
*.jsonl
!tests/fixtures/agent_stream/*.jsonl
boot.log
eagle.log

# Marks a checkout install.sh made, which `eagle update` may reset.
.aethelark-install

# Local, uncommitted instructions and notes for whoever works here.
CLAUDE.local.md
